  "title": "Places in town",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "Mosques (**মসজিদ**) are everywhere in Bangladesh and make the handiest landmarks: **মসজিদের সামনে** 'in front of the mosque'. Hindu temples (**মন্দির**) are common too, especially in older neighbourhoods. People often give their address this way — a road name plus 'next to the market' (**বাজারের পাশে**) or 'near the mosque' (**মসজিদের কাছে**).\n\n'Live' (in a place) is **থাকি** (I) / **থাকেন** (you or he/she, polite):\n\n- **আমি শহরে থাকি** — 'I live in the city'\n- **আমার নানি গ্রামে থাকেন** — 'My grandmother lives in the village' (polite **-েন** for an elder)\n- **আপনি কোথায় থাকেন?** — 'Where do you live?' (polite)\n\n**আছে** at the end of a sentence means 'there is': **মসজিদের সামনে একটা দোকান আছে** 'There's a shop in front of the mosque'.\n\n**Culture:** most city families still have a village home — the **বাড়ি** from Lesson 1 — and go back to their **গ্রাম** for Eid. Dhaka empties out as millions travel home.",
